        <p class="eyebrow">Folio Documentation</p>
        <h1 class="docs-title">Learn Folio</h1>
        <p class="docs-lead">
          Write Markdown, preview real pages, and export polished documents. Learn the editor, portable files and history, document layouts, reusable styles, and local agent workflows.
        </p>
        <p class="tip-callout">This guide follows the current source. The <a href={`${base}/changelog/`}>changelog</a> identifies features available in released builds and those coming in the next release.</p>
        <details class="docs-mobile-toc">
          <summary>On this page</summary>
          <nav aria-label="Documentation contents">
            {contents.map(section => <a href={`#${section.id}`}>{section.label}</a>)}
          </nav>
        </details>
      </section>

      <!-- 1. GETTING STARTED -->
      <section id="getting-started" class="docs-section">
        <h2 class="section-heading">1. Getting Started</h2>
        <p>
          Folio is a macOS-first desktop application engineered with Electron, React, CodeMirror 6, and Paged.js. It bridges the gap between lightweight Markdown writing and executive-ready A4 PDF publishing.
        </p>

        <h3 class="sub-heading">Installation</h3>
        <p>Download the official release for your operating system:</p>
        <div class="download-chips">
          <a href={downloadSilicon} class="chip-btn" id="docs-mac-arm">
            <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 2v20M17 5H9.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 0 0 7h5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 0 7H6"/></svg>
            <span>macOS (Apple Silicon .dmg)</span>
          </a>
          <a href={downloadIntel} class="chip-btn secondary" id="docs-mac-intel">
            <span>macOS (Intel .dmg)</span>
          </a>
          <a href={downloadWinExe} class="chip-btn secondary" id="docs-win-exe">
            <span>Windows (Installer .exe)</span>
          </a>
          <a href={downloadLinuxAppImage} class="chip-btn secondary" id="docs-linux-appimage">
            <span>Linux (.AppImage)</span>
          </a>
          <a href={downloadLinuxDeb} class="chip-btn secondary" id="docs-linux-deb">
            <span>Linux (.deb)</span>
          </a>
        </div>
        <p class="tip-callout">
          <strong>Tip:</strong> On macOS, drag <strong>Folio.app</strong> into <code>/Applications</code>. On Windows, run the installer <code>.exe</code>. On Linux, make the <code>.AppImage</code> executable with <code>chmod +x</code> or install the <code>.deb</code> package.
        </p>

        <h3 id="editor-tour" class="sub-heading">Editor Interface & Shortcuts</h3>
        <p>Write Markdown on the left and preview paginated A4 pages on the right. Three toolbar controls select editor-only, split, or preview-only view. Drag the divider to resize the panes, use its arrow keys when focused, or double-click it to reset the width. In split view, the preview follows your current paragraph; clicking a preview paragraph moves the editor caret to its source.</p>
        <p>Choose <strong>New</strong> for starters such as Scientific Article and Landscape Presentation, or explore the Petstore API Documentation sample with endpoints, authentication, code, and a Mermaid integration diagram.</p>
        <p>These shortcuts use Command on macOS. Use Ctrl in place of Command on Windows and Linux.</p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="docs-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Shortcut</th>
                <th>Action</th>
                <th>Description</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><code>⌘ O</code></td>
                <td>Open File</td>
                <td>Open a local <code>.md</code>, <code>.markdown</code>, or <code>.folio</code> file.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>⌘ S</code></td>
                <td>Save File</td>
                <td>Saves the current document; Folio files also keep saved versions.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>⇧ ⌘ E</code></td>
                <td>Export PDF</td>
                <td>Renders and compiles the paginated document into a vector PDF.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>⌘ F</code></td>
                <td>Find & Replace</td>
                <td>Opens the CodeMirror search bar in the editor pane.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>⇧ ⌘ S</code></td>
                <td>Save As</td>
                <td>Save the document to a new path or change its file format.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>⌘ K</code></td>
                <td>Insert Link</td>
                <td>Open the link dialog; a URL pasted over selected text also creates a link.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>⌘ ,</code></td>
                <td>Settings</td>
                <td>Change interface appearance, language, editor preferences, and autosave.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3 id="editing-tools" class="sub-heading">Writing, Tables & Images</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Formatting:</strong> Choose headings H1–H6, paragraphs, emphasis, lists, and quotes from the toolbar. Find opens search and replace; Outline jumps to document headings.</li>
          <li><strong>Tables:</strong> Use Insert → Table to choose rows and columns. Tab and Shift+Tab move between cells; Tab in the last cell adds a row. The contextual Table menu adds or removes rows and columns.</li>
          <li><strong>Lists and tasks:</strong> Enter continues a list, and Enter on an empty item ends it. Tab and Shift+Tab indent or outdent. Tasks inserts Markdown checkboxes, and the task control toggles their state.</li>
          <li><strong>Footnotes:</strong> Insert → Footnote adds a reference and definition. Standard <code>[^note]</code> references render numbered notes at the end of the document.</li>
          <li><strong>Code:</strong> Add a language after the opening fence, such as <code>typescript</code>, <code>python</code>, or <code>sql</code>. The editor, preview, and PDF use syntax colors; unsupported languages stay plain code. Long lines wrap within print margins.</li>
          <li><strong>Images:</strong> Paste, drop, or choose a file through Insert → Image, then add alt text. Place the caret inside an image or link to edit it through the toolbar. Image captions use the Markdown title; an optional width suffix controls sizing.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="code-block"><div class="code-header"><span>Image caption and width</span></div><pre><code>![Architecture diagram](architecture.png "System overview")&#123;width=50%&#125;</code></pre></div>
        <p>Pasted images stay in draft storage until you save. Saving Markdown places imported images in a neighboring <code>.assets/</code> folder; keep it with the Markdown file. Save As copies imported assets when moving the document. Existing relative image paths are supported.</p>

        <h3 id="settings" class="sub-heading">Settings & Language</h3>
        <p>Open Settings for appearance, 🇺🇸 English or 🇪🇸 Español, line numbers, folding, current-line and preview-selection highlights, font size, line spacing, text width, spellcheck, and autosave. Language is an app preference: changing it leaves document content unchanged. Legacy <code>lang</code> frontmatter is ignored.</p>
        <p>Autosave applies to existing files after a pause in typing. Unsaved drafts can be recovered after restart. If a file changes outside Folio, autosave pauses and offers Reload, Save my version, or Save As.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="folio-documents" class="docs-section">
        <h2 class="section-heading">2. Files, History & Sharing</h2>
        <p>Use Save options → <strong>Save as Folio document…</strong> to create a portable <code>.folio</code> file. It contains Markdown, local images and attachments, the document's appearance, named styles, and up to 100 saved versions. Open it like an ordinary document.</p>
        <p>Markdown stays supported. Save As can convert a Folio document to <code>.md</code>, placing its assets beside it in <code>.assets/</code> and retaining supported font and color overrides in frontmatter.</p>

        <h3 id="document-history" class="sub-heading">Saved Versions</h3>
        <p>The history button opens saved versions, including their text, assets, and styles. Restoring a version creates an unsaved draft; save it to record a new version. Unchanged saves do not create duplicate versions, and Folio retains the latest 100.</p>
        <p>Choose <strong>Save a copy without history…</strong> in the history dialog to share only the current document and assets.</p>

        <h3 id="export-sharing" class="sub-heading">PDF & Markdown Export</h3>
        <p><strong>Export PDF</strong> uses the paginated layout shown in the preview, including orientation, headers, footers, tables, math, and diagrams. The arrow beside it offers <strong>Markdown + assets (.zip)</strong>, which packages the current draft and local attachments with portable paths. Extract the ZIP before opening its Markdown file.</p>
        <p>PDF and Markdown ZIP export work with both Markdown and Folio documents. ZIP export contains the current document and assets without revision history. Missing local files are reported before a package is produced.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- FRONT MATTER -->
      <section id="frontmatter" class="docs-section">
        <h2 class="section-heading">3. Document Layouts & Frontmatter</h2>
        <p>
          Folio documents are 100% standard Markdown. You can optionally include a YAML frontmatter block at the very top of your document to configure layouts, metadata, invoices, or running headers and footers.
        </p>
        <p>Open <strong>Document</strong> for Document properties: choose a layout and page orientation, edit layout-specific metadata, and configure the table of contents, headers, and footers. These choices are saved in frontmatter.</p>

        <h3 id="layout-report" class="sub-heading">Cover Report / SOW (<code>layout: report</code>)</h3>
        <p>
          The default layout for statements of work, proposals, whitepapers, and formal reports. Generates a branded cover page with metadata fields, followed by numbered pages.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">
          <div class="code-header"><span>sample-sow.md</span></div>
          <pre><code>---
layout: report
title: Enterprise Cloud Architecture Proposal
kicker: Technical Proposal
client: Acme Global Industries
prepared-for: Marcus Vance, CTO
prepared-by: Meridian Cloud Advisory
date: October 12, 2026
valid-until: November 12, 2026
document-id: SOW-2026-089
theme: editorial
---

# Executive Summary

This proposal outlines the multi-region migration strategy...</code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3 id="layout-invoice" class="sub-heading">Invoices (<code>layout: invoice</code>)</h3>
        <p>Use flat frontmatter fields for issuer/client details, dates, status, and totals. Write line items as a Markdown table in the body. Folio displays the supplied amounts; it does not calculate totals from the table.</p>
        <div class="code-block">
          <div class="code-header"><span>invoice-0042.md</span></div>
          <pre><code>---
layout: invoice
title: Invoice INV-2026-0042
invoice-number: INV-2026-0042
date: 2026-10-13
due-date: 2026-11-13
status: Due
from: Example Studio
from-email: billing@example.com
client: Northstar Technologies
currency: USD
subtotal: "$4,500.00"
tax: "$360.00"
tax-rate: "8%"
total: "$4,860.00"
payment-terms: Net 30 days
theme: editorial
---

### Services

| Description | Hours | Rate | Amount |
|:---|---:|---:|---:|
| Architecture consulting | 30 | $150.00 | $4,500.00 |

### Payment Instructions

Contact billing@example.com for payment details.</code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3 id="layout-simple" class="sub-heading">Simple Documents & Running Headers/Footers (<code>layout: simple</code>)</h3>
        <p>
          Ideal for memos, technical specs, meeting notes, articles, and short policies where a cover page is unnecessary. You can customize running headers and footers directly:
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">
          <div class="code-header"><span>tech-spec.md</span></div>
          <pre><code>---
layout: simple
title: Real-Time Sync Engine Spec
header-left: "Folio Engine V2"
header-right: "Confidential"
footer-left: "Revision 1.4"
footer-right: "Page &#123;page&#125; of &#123;pages&#125;"
theme: graphite
---

# Architecture Overview

This specification details the conflict-free replicated data types...</code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3 id="layout-scientific" class="sub-heading">Scientific Articles (<code>layout: scientific</code>)</h3>
        <p>Choose New → Scientific Article for a manuscript starter, or select Scientific Article in Document properties. The article starts on the first page with a title, authors, affiliations, abstract, and keywords, followed by compact research sections. It supports ordinary Markdown equations, figures, footnotes, tables, and references.</p>
        <div class="code-block"><div class="code-header"><span>article.md</span></div><pre><code>---
layout: scientific
theme: scientific
title: Reproducible State Estimation
authors: [Alex Rivera, Sam Chen]
affiliations:
  - Example Research Lab
abstract: |
  A comparison of two state-estimation methods.
keywords: [estimation, reproducibility]
journal: Example Journal
doi: "10.xxxx/example"
---

## 1. Introduction

Research question and contribution.</code></pre></div>
        <p>The Scientific theme is the default for this layout when no theme is specified. Dates and publication details appear when supplied; headers and a table of contents are optional. The MCP preset is <code>scientific-paper</code>.</p>

        <h3 id="layout-cv" class="sub-heading">Curriculum Vitae (<code>layout: cv</code>)</h3>
        <p>Choose New → Curriculum Vitae for a resume starter. Document properties offers name, role, contact details, location, and website fields. Use Markdown headings for experience, education, and other sections.</p>

        <h3 id="landscape-pages" class="sub-heading">Landscape Pages</h3>
        <p>Document properties → Page orientation selects Portrait or Landscape for the entire document, including report covers and PDF export. The setting is stored as <code>orientation: landscape</code>. New → Landscape Presentation starts a wide document with page breaks between sections.</p>
        <div class="code-block"><pre><code>---
layout: simple
orientation: landscape
title: Quarterly Review
theme: slate
---

# Quarterly Review

Opening summary.

&lt;!-- pagebreak --&gt;

## Delivery Metrics</code></pre></div>

        <h3 id="mermaid-katex" class="sub-heading">Mermaid Diagrams & KaTeX Math</h3>
        <p>
          Folio natively paginates Mermaid diagrams and LaTeX equations:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Mermaid:</strong> Use standard fenced <code>```mermaid</code> blocks. Folio colors them according to your active theme swatch!</li>
          <li><strong>LaTeX:</strong> Use <code>$E = mc^2$</code> or <code>\(...\)</code> inline. Put display delimiters <code>$$</code> or <code>\[</code> and <code>\]</code> on their own lines. Fenced <code>math</code> blocks also work. Insert offers editable inline and display examples.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="code-block"><pre><code>$$
\frac&#123;\partial f&#125;&#123;\partial x&#125; = 2x
$$</code></pre></div>
        <p>Equations use bundled KaTeX fonts in preview and PDF. Escape a literal dollar sign as <code>\$</code> when it could be mistaken for a math delimiter. Math support is for equations inside Markdown; complete <code>.tex</code> documents are not imported.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- 3. CUSTOMIZING THEMES -->
      <section id="customizing-themes" class="docs-section">
        <h2 class="section-heading">4. Styles & Typography</h2>
        <p>
          Folio bundles 7 curated design presets crafted specifically for print pagination:
        </p>

        <div class="themes-overview-grid">
          <div class="theme-pill"><strong>voltage</strong>: Pitch black, acid neon lime, sharp monospace</div>
          <div class="theme-pill"><strong>editorial</strong>: British racing green cover, terracotta rules, serif type</div>
          <div class="theme-pill"><strong>slate</strong>: Midnight navy, sky blue accents, corporate engineering</div>
          <div class="theme-pill"><strong>scientific</strong>: Paper white, formal steel navy, numbered math</div>
          <div class="theme-pill"><strong>contrast</strong>: Brutalist monochrome black & white, high contrast</div>
          <div class="theme-pill"><strong>graphite</strong>: Charcoal gray, technical drafting lime</div>
          <div class="theme-pill"><strong>nocturne</strong>: Deep plum, cyberpunk violet & pink</div>
        </div>

        <h3 id="saved-styles" class="sub-heading">Create & Save Styles</h3>
        <p>Choose <strong>Edit styles…</strong> in the style picker, or open the inspector's Style tab. Font choices show live previews, and the main color controls update your document immediately. More options contains the detailed palette, including code syntax colors.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Duplicate style</strong> starts from the current theme and edits.</li>
          <li><strong>New style</strong> starts from a built-in theme.</li>
          <li><strong>Save style changes</strong> updates the named style; save the document to persist its library.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Named styles appear under My styles in the picker. They are stored inside the <code>.folio</code> document along with the active style and revision snapshots. A Markdown draft with named styles offers a Folio save so the library is retained; exporting Markdown carries the applied font/color overrides. Previously saved app styles remain available and are copied into a document when used.</p>
        <p class="tip-callout">Reusable named styles and their MCP tools are listed under <strong>Next release</strong> in the <a href={`${base}/changelog/`}>changelog</a>.</p>

        <h3 id="theme-overrides" class="sub-heading">Per-Document Overrides</h3>
        <p>
          You can override any theme's color palette or font families inside your Markdown frontmatter:
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">
          <div class="code-header"><span>frontmatter-overrides.yaml</span></div>
          <pre><code>---
theme: editorial
theme-overrides:
  heading: "#2563eb"
  cover-accent: "#2563eb"
  font-display: "Space Grotesk, sans-serif"
  font-body: "Inter, sans-serif"
  font-code: "JetBrains Mono, monospace"
  font-mono: "JetBrains Mono, monospace"
---</code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3 id="print-styles" class="sub-heading">Page Breaks and Landscape Sections</h3>
        <p>Use Insert → Page break, or put <code>&lt;!-- pagebreak --&gt;</code> on its own line. For an individual wide section inside a portrait document, use the landscape comments below on separate lines:</p>
        <div class="code-block">
          <pre><code>&lt;!-- Force a clean page break before a new section --&gt;
&lt;!-- pagebreak --&gt;

&lt;!-- Rotate a wide financial table or diagram into landscape mode --&gt;
&lt;!-- landscape --&gt;

| Q1 Revenue | Q2 Revenue | Q3 Revenue | Q4 Revenue | Total YTD | Margin |
|:---|:---|:---|:---|:---|:---|
| $1,200,000 | $1,450,000 | $1,800,000 | $2,100,000 | $6,550,000 | 38.2% |

&lt;!-- /landscape --&gt;</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 4. MCP INTEGRATION -->
      <section id="mcp" class="docs-section">
        <h2 class="section-heading">5. Model Context Protocol (MCP) Integration</h2>
        <p>
          Folio includes a native <strong>Model Context Protocol (MCP)</strong> server. This allows AI coding agents and LLM clients (such as Claude Desktop, Cursor, Google Antigravity, Cline, and Windsurf) to inspect document schemas, create branded documents, customize frontmatter, lint formatting, and compile pixel-perfect PDFs end-to-end.
        </p>

        <h3 id="mcp-flag" class="sub-heading">Zero-Clone Connection via Native App Flag (<code>--mcp</code>)</h3>
        <p>
          You do <strong>not</strong> need to clone this repository, install Node.js, or hardcode development paths. If you have <strong>Folio.app</strong> installed in your Applications folder, launch it directly in headless MCP mode:
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">
          <div class="code-header"><span>Terminal command</span></div>
          <pre><code>/Applications/Folio.app/Contents/MacOS/Folio --mcp</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p class="tip-callout">
          <strong>Pro Tip: Create a system-wide <code>folio</code> CLI shortcut</strong><br/>
          Run this one-line command in your terminal to symlink Folio to your PATH:
          <br/><br/>
          <code>sudo ln -sf /Applications/Folio.app/Contents/MacOS/Folio /usr/local/bin/folio</code>
          <br/><br/>
          Then you can simply configure your AI tools to run <code>folio --mcp</code> anywhere!
        </p>

        <h3 id="mcp-clients" class="sub-heading">Client Configuration Snippets</h3>
        <p>Copy and paste the configuration block for your preferred AI client:</p>

        <div class="client-tabs">
          <div class="client-tab">
            <h4>Claude Desktop</h4>
            <p>File: <code>~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json</code></p>
            <div class="code-block">
              <pre><code>&#123;
  "mcpServers": &#123;
    "folio": &#123;
      "command": "/Applications/Folio.app/Contents/MacOS/Folio",
      "args": ["--mcp"]
    &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="client-tab">
            <h4>Cursor</h4>
            <p>File: <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code> or <strong>Settings &gt; Features &gt; MCP</strong></p>
            <div class="code-block">
              <pre><code>&#123;
  "mcpServers": &#123;
    "folio": &#123;
      "command": "/Applications/Folio.app/Contents/MacOS/Folio",
      "args": ["--mcp"]
    &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="client-tab">
            <h4>Google Antigravity</h4>
            <p>File: <code>~/.gemini/config/mcp_config.json</code></p>
            <div class="code-block">
              <pre><code>&#123;
  "mcpServers": &#123;
    "folio": &#123;
      "command": "/Applications/Folio.app/Contents/MacOS/Folio",
      "args": ["--mcp"]
    &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 id="mcp-tools" class="sub-heading">The 12 MCP Agent Tools</h3>
        <p>The document tools read and write Markdown. The five style tools operate on an existing <code>.folio</code> file saved in the app. This reference follows the current source; see the changelog for released availability.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="docs-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Tool Name</th>
                <th>Purpose</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><code>folio_list_document_types</code></td>
                <td>Lists 10 built-in presets, including scientific-paper, plus custom workspace presets. No inputs.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>folio_get_document_type</code></td>
                <td>Takes <code>typeId</code>; returns fields, defaults, starter content, supported placeholders, and editable theme tokens.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>folio_create_document</code></td>
                <td>Takes required <code>title</code> and <code>content</code>; optional <code>type</code>, <code>theme</code>, <code>metadata</code>, <code>themeOverrides</code>, and <code>outputPath</code>. Returns Markdown and lint findings.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>folio_customize_document</code></td>
                <td>Takes <code>source</code> or <code>filePath</code>, with optional <code>setLayout</code>, <code>setTheme</code>, <code>setMetadata</code>, <code>setThemeOverrides</code>, <code>setHeader</code>, <code>setFooter</code>, and <code>outputPath</code>.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>folio_lint_document</code></td>
                <td>Takes <code>source</code> or <code>filePath</code>; checks fences, landscape markers, local heading anchors, tables, and frontmatter guidance. Returns counts and <code>findings</code>.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>folio_export_pdf</code></td>
                <td>Takes <code>inputPath</code> or <code>source</code>, required <code>outputPath</code>, and optional <code>theme</code>/<code>lang</code> export overrides. Renders portrait or landscape PDF pages.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>folio_register_document_type</code></td>
                <td>Takes required <code>id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>description</code>, and <code>baseLayout</code>; optional defaults, headers/footers, fields, and <code>starterTemplate</code>. Saves to <code>.folio/presets/&lt;id&gt;.json</code>.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr><td><code>folio_list_styles</code></td><td>Reads named document styles, the active style, font choices, and editable tokens from a .folio file.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>folio_create_style</code></td><td>Creates a named style inside the document from a base theme and overrides.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>folio_duplicate_style</code></td><td>Copies a named style or the current document appearance.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>folio_update_style</code></td><td>Updates a saved style without applying it to the document.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>folio_apply_style</code></td><td>Applies a document style while preserving attachments and recording a restorable revision.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Pass <code>outputPath</code> to create/customize when you want to save the returned Markdown. Customizing a file without an output path returns changes without writing them; use the same input and output path to overwrite it. Metadata updates may rewrite YAML comments. Supply an empty <code>content</code> string to create from the preset's starter body.</p>
        <p>Style tools take a required <code>filePath</code>. List styles to discover IDs and available font/color tokens. Create, duplicate, and update change the library; apply changes the document's appearance. Style mutations preserve attachments and record a restorable revision when something changes. Updates to an open file use the editor's external-change/reload flow.</p>
        <p>For <code>folio_create_style</code>, supply <code>name</code>, <code>theme</code>, and optional <code>overrides</code>. Duplicate accepts optional <code>styleId</code> and <code>name</code>. Update requires <code>styleId</code> with optional <code>name</code>, <code>theme</code>, and <code>overrides</code>; its override map replaces the saved map. Apply requires <code>styleId</code>.</p>

        <h3 class="sub-heading">Create a Scientific Article</h3>
        <p>Inspect <code>scientific-paper</code> with <code>folio_get_document_type</code>, then call <code>folio_create_document</code> with these arguments:</p>
        <div class="code-block"><div class="code-header"><span>folio_create_document arguments</span></div><pre><code>&#123;
  "type": "scientific-paper",
  "title": "Reproducible State Estimation",
  "metadata": &#123;
    "authors": ["Alex Rivera", "Sam Chen"],
    "affiliations": ["Example Research Lab"],
    "orientation": "portrait"
  &#125;,
  "content": "## 1. Introduction\n\nResearch question and contribution.\n",
  "outputPath": "./article.md"
&#125;</code></pre></div>
        <p>Lint with <code>folio_lint_document</code> using <code>filePath: "./article.md"</code>, then export with <code>folio_export_pdf</code> using <code>inputPath: "./article.md"</code> and <code>outputPath: "./article.pdf"</code>. Set <code>metadata.orientation</code> to <code>landscape</code> for a wide document. Layouts are <code>report</code>, <code>simple</code>, <code>invoice</code>, <code>cv</code>, and <code>scientific</code>.</p>
        <p>To export a saved style through MCP, apply it to the existing Folio file, then pass the returned <code>markdown</code> as <code>source</code> to lint and PDF export. The create/export document tools do not directly read or create Folio archives.</p>
        <p>See the <a href="https://github.com/carlosrivera/folio/blob/main/docs/mcp.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">complete MCP reference</a> for all parameter names and examples.</p>

        <h3 id="mcp-presets" class="sub-heading">Custom Presets in <code>.folio/presets/</code></h3>
        <p>
          Registering a custom document format saves it into <code>.folio/presets/&lt;id&gt;.json</code> under the MCP server's working directory. Start the server in your workspace to discover that workspace's presets.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 5. DEVELOPMENT -->
      <section id="development" class="docs-section">
        <h2 class="section-heading">6. Development & Contributing</h2>
        <p>
          Contributing to Folio or running the app from source is straightforward:
        </p>

        <h3 class="sub-heading">Prerequisites</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>macOS</strong> (Apple Silicon or Intel)</li>
          <li><strong>Node.js 22.12</strong> or newer</li>
          <li><strong>pnpm 11+</strong> (<code>corepack enable &amp;&amp; corepack prepare pnpm@latest --activate</code>)</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="sub-heading">Cloning & Running Locally</h3>
        <div class="code-block">
          <pre><code># 1. Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/carlosrivera/folio.git
cd folio

# 2. Install dependencies
pnpm install

# 3. Start development environment
pnpm dev</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p>
          <code>pnpm dev</code> starts the Vite renderer server on <code>localhost:5173</code> and launches the Electron application with hot reloading.
        </p>

        <h3 id="running-tests" class="sub-heading">Running Tests & Verification</h3>
        <div class="code-block">
          <pre><code># Run Vitest unit tests (lib, diff, document parser, linting, mcp server)
pnpm test

# Run full project verification (TypeScript + Vitest + Render Tests + Editor Smoke)
pnpm check

# Run headless golden visual regression tests
pnpm test:render

# Test MCP server directly over stdio
pnpm mcp</code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3 id="packaging-app" class="sub-heading">Packaging macOS Releases Locally</h3>
        <p>
          Folio uses <code>electron-builder</code> to produce separate Apple Silicon and Intel apps, disk images, and ZIP archives:
        </p>
        <div class="code-block">
          <pre><code># Build macOS distributables (.dmg and .zip) into dist-packaged.noindex/
pnpm dist:mac

# Build unpackaged directory app for inspection
pnpm dist:dir</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p>For a signed release, <code>FOLIO_NOTARY_PROFILE=ocelo-notary pnpm release:mac:local</code> uses the configured Keychain credentials, verifies both app bundles, and notarizes and staples the signed disk images. This requires the project's signing/notarization setup.</p>
        <p>Create the GitHub release draft, then run <code>pnpm release:mac:upload</code> to upload the verified Mac artifacts. The upload checks remote sizes and SHA-256 hashes before removing that version's local staging files; failed uploads keep local output. Build output uses <code>dist-packaged.noindex/</code> to keep staging copies out of Spotlight.</p>

        <h3 class="sub-heading">Landing Page & Docs Development</h3>
        <div class="code-block">
          <pre><code># Run Astro docs & landing server locally
pnpm landing:dev

# Build static production site to landing/dist/
pnpm landing:build

# Preview the production build
pnpm landing:preview</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p>Pushes to <code>main</code> deploy the website through GitHub Pages. Documentation lives at <code>/folio/docs/</code>; shared navigation uses the same directory URL on desktop and mobile.</p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>

  <!-- Footer -->
  <SiteFooter />

  <script>
    import { macDownloadArchitecture, type MacArchitectureHints } from '../lib/mac-architecture'
    const ua = navigator.userAgent || ''
    const platform = (navigator as any).userAgentData?.platform || navigator.platform || ''
    const armBtn = document.getElementById('docs-mac-arm')
    const intelBtn = document.getElementById('docs-mac-intel')
    const winBtn = document.getElementById('docs-win-exe')
    const linuxBtn = document.getElementById('docs-linux-appimage')

    function setActiveBtn(active: HTMLElement | null) {
      ;[armBtn, intelBtn, winBtn, linuxBtn].forEach((btn) => btn?.classList.add('secondary'))
      active?.classList.remove('secondary')
    }

    async function selectDownload() {
      if (/Win/i.test(ua) || /Win/i.test(platform)) {
        setActiveBtn(winBtn)
      } else if (/Linux/i.test(ua) || /Linux/i.test(platform)) {
        setActiveBtn(linuxBtn)
      } else {
        const architecture = await macDownloadArchitecture(navigator as Navigator & MacArchitectureHints)
        setActiveBtn(architecture === 'x64' ? intelBtn : armBtn)
      }
    }
    void selectDownload()
  </script>
